Z||000000||Brian84SE||03-07-2000||09:20 PM||||Fiero 56:  I romoved the thermostat.<BR>It was 60 degrees today and it stayed between  the 2nd line and 220. (170-180?) This was for an extended ride on the highway.<P>So, cooling system is working properly, right?  Could the thermostat be binding in the housing?  I am still going to put the 180 thermostat in.||||reg||1
Z||000001||fierospeeder||03-07-2000||10:01 PM||||is this an example of how its suppose to work.<P>Say you have a 180 temp thermostat.  Your coolant will be around 180 degrees while driving, if it gets hotter, the thermostat will open up.  When you are at a stoplight, you will notice the gauge reading a higher temperature, at around a certain temp, such as 200, your fan will kick on and cool your system until a certain temp.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||03-07-2000||11:20 PM||||Thermostats commonly malfunction, even if they're not very old. If there is any doubt, buy a new one, they're cheap.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||03-08-2000||09:00 AM||||Running without a thermostat, that sounds a little high. When I would run without mine on the highway, the temp needle stayed just a hair above the cold line, never went up. But this was with a v-6, not sure how the temps would change between the two engines.  Sounds like the radiator may be starting to plug, or Ogre's suggestion on air flow. It wouldn't hurt to try another new thermostat though. Eliminates some guesswork.<P>Fierospeeder:<BR>it works something like that, I don't know the exact specifics. Once the temps around here, get to be staying in the 80s, then I'll toss the 180 in, seems to really help when in slow traffic. The fan is designed to come on at 235, too high for my tastes, you could change out the fan sensor, or wire in a manual switch for it. I wired in a switch.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 03-08-2000).]||||reg||1
